The Explainable AI extractions for Dataset 3 demonstrate that the Tri-Model-Fusion architecture has successfully mapped the intersection of severe somatic symptoms, occupational burnout, and acute psychological distress. The global word-to-word markers vividly capture the physical manifestations of anxiety and insomnia, mathematically linking concepts like physical "twitches," medical anxieties ("surgory"), and staying awake "till" the "sun." Yet, the model's true clinical sophistication shines in the semantic document-to-document bridges, which flawlessly cluster distinct behavioral archetypes: it paired tweets expressing severe lifestyle exhaustion and occupational burnout (getting three hours of sleep bridged with taking an FMLA leave of absence), matched vulnerable newcomers tentatively seeking community support ("feeling kind lost" bridged with "searching internet"), and directly connected chronic, long-term suicidal ideation with visceral descriptions of intense internal emotional dysregulation. This proves that the semantic Cosine edges are dynamically categorizing patients not just by general sadness, but by highly specific clinical vulnerabilities and real-world functional impairments.


